/-!
# Walls of 22 cells beside traps carried far along a background's columns

A trap carried along the columns of a background periodic in time fixes its first cells to that background's columns
([ElevenTraps](ElevenTraps.md), [FourteenTraps](FourteenTraps.md)). In the game of 22 cells some of these traps fail the
test of reach while almost every node can reach them: the few nodes that cannot hold a closed set of windows, and on it
column 1 is fixed at the white phases, so it is a trap of the same block, a wall that the census of locked windows of
16 cells does not see. The carried trap and its wall are apart from each other, so entry into either alone is false.
This module checks each wall as a trap, refutes entry into each of the two from the other (`not_absorption_fwd` of
[ReachRefuted](ReachRefuted.md)), and reduces the block to entry into one of the two (`WTrap.AbsorptionSome` of
[TrapPairs](TrapPairs.md)).

* `wide_<trap>`, with `_check` and `_closed`: the wall found beside the carried trap `<trap>`, of 22 cells.
* `wide_<trap>_cut`, `_holds`, `_avoids`, `<trap>_not_absorption_fwd`: the states of the carried trap cut to 22 cells,
  that the wall avoids them, and the refutation of entry into the carried trap.
* `<trap>_closed_wide`, `wide_<trap>_apart`, `_holds`, `_avoids`, `wide_<trap>_not_absorption`: the states of the
  wall, that the carried trap avoids them, and the refutation of entry into the wall.
* `widePair_<block>`, `widePair_<block>_excludes`: the carried trap and its wall, and the reduction of the block to
  entry into one of the two.
* `wideWalls`, `wideWalls_check`, `wideWalls_blocks`: the list of the walls, its check and its blocks.

The tables come from `python -m computations.lock_chain --lean-wide-walls`. The natural-language argument is
`WideWalls.md`, beside this file.
-/

@[expose] public section

-- Sequential elaboration: the kernel checks would otherwise run in parallel and stack their memory.
set_option Elab.async false

